the same resources and the same technology, why on earth would one opt for aggressive

13
00:00:57,520 --> 00:01:02,320
taxation and the other plunge into the free market? Well, the document we are analyzing

14
00:01:02,320 --> 00:01:06,880
suggests that the answer is not found simply in the numbers, but rather in the historical

15
00:01:06,880 --> 00:01:11,960
lenses through which a society views its bank accounts. So let's get into the heart of it

16
00:01:11,960 --> 00:01:17,800
with the first section: what exactly is unfair inequality? The central premise

17
00:01:17,800 --> 00:01:22,960
of this study introduces a concept that really changes the game. The distributive

18
00:01:22,960 --> 00:01:27,600
policies adopted by a government are not simply dictated by how much inequality

19
00:01:27,600 --> 00:01:32,840
exists within the country. Rather, everything revolves around how society perceives

20
00:01:32,840 --> 00:01:37,520
that inequality. To build their mathematical model, the authors make a rigorous,

21
00:01:37,520 --> 00:01:42,400
almost surgical distinction. On one hand, we have so-called fair wealth, which is that

22
00:01:42,400 --> 00:01:47,080
wealth generated exclusively through innate talent and hard work. On the other,

23
00:01:47,080 --> 00:01:51,480
we find unfair wealth, born of pure luck, inherited privileges,

24
00:01:51,480 --> 00:01:56,359
the right connections, or in some cases, state intervention. Essentially, an income

25
00:01:56,359 --> 00:02:00,800
disparity arising from grueling work shifts is willingly accepted, while

26
00:02:00,800 --> 00:02:04,719
the same disparity resulting from favoritism, well, absolutely not.

27
00:02:04,719 --> 00:02:09,400
And here, things get really interesting because the model reveals a paradox, a true

28
00:02:09,400 --> 00:02:14,439
self-reinforcing cycle with profound consequences. Let's imagine the scene: we start

29
00:02:14,439 --> 00:02:18,560
from a strong perception of injustice within society. To correct this

30
00:02:18,560 --> 00:02:22,560
anomaly, democratic governments decide to drastically raise tax rates.

31
00:02:22,560 --> 00:02:27,280
But beware, by heavily taxing profits, they end up discouraging individual effort.

32
00:02:27,280 --> 00:02:31,719
People simply work less because it is no longer worth it. The ironic result

33
00:02:31,719 --> 00:02:36,360
of all this, with effort reduced to a minimum, luck and inherited wealth

34
00:02:36,360 --> 00:02:40,920
become the primary, if not the only, driver of wealth, making society even

35
00:02:40,920 --> 00:02:46,400
more dependent on chance than it was in the beginning. A crazy chain reaction.

36
00:02:46,400 --> 00:02:51,640
Let's now move on to Section 2, an interesting macroeconomic comparison between the United States and Europe.

37
00:02:51,640 --> 00:02:56,840
To turn this theory into something more tangible, let's do a little thought experiment.

38
00:02:56,840 --> 00:03:01,800
The authors use it to calibrate their model, directly comparing two continents

39
00:03:01,800 --> 00:03:06,400
at the moment of their full democratization. Let's assume that Europe and the United States

40
00:03:06,400 --> 00:03:10,960
had the exact same level of total inequality at the dawn of their modern democracies.

41
00:03:10,960 --> 00:03:16,000
The weight of history, however, was undeniably different. Europe carried the burden of centuries

42
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:20,000
of feudalism, nobility, and rigidly separated social classes.

43
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:24,759
Consequently, European citizens perceived almost all inequality as deeply unfair.

44
00:03:24,759 --> 00:03:28,400
On the other side of the ocean, the United States boasted a younger capitalist history,

45
00:03:28,400 --> 00:03:32,080
where wealth was seen as the fruit of merit, frontier spirit,

46
00:03:32,080 --> 00:03:36,479
and entrepreneurship. In short, wealth perceived as largely fair.

47
00:03:36,479 --> 00:03:42,240
And the simulation data clearly show how this initial historical perception

48
00:03:42,240 --> 00:03:48,240
then dictated the economic future of both sides of the Atlantic.

49
